From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:19:04 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(egfx): bound what waits for the external H.264 decoder

With an external decoder, the commands after an H.264 frame wait for
its pixels, and the pixels of frames decoded ahead of their turn wait
for the frames before them. Nothing bounded either. A server could send
a frame the decoder holds on to, then any number of commands behind it,
while every frame decoded meanwhile kept its pixels (up to 256 MiB
each) in the WebAssembly memory that all of a page's sessions share,
until it ran out; only the decoder's watchdog (3 s without a picture)
stopped that. And each completion looked for its frame through the
whole queue, so the completions behind a long queue took quadratic
time.

What waits is now bounded at all times, whatever the timing:

- At most 65,536 commands, frames included. A frame holds a handful of
  commands, a full repaint from the bitmap cache one per 64x64 tile
  (2,040 for a 4K screen), and servers pace themselves by the frame
  acknowledgements, which wait too.
- At most 512 MiB of commands (as sent), regions, access units not yet
  taken, and pixels decoded ahead of their turn: twice what the
  compositor holds, or four whole 8K pictures (127 MiB each) waiting
  behind a fifth.

Both are checked as each command is queued and as each frame completes,
and the command or completion that goes past either fails `process` or
`complete_h264_frame`, which ends the session. For that, the commands
of a message are queued one at a time, each processed (or left
waiting) before the next is queued: a message holds up to 256 MiB of
them, and handing out a frame allocates, so a check at its end could
come after any number of them. A frame's acknowledgement therefore
counts the frames after it in the same message only once they wait,
no longer as soon as the message arrives.

A completion now finds its frame in constant time: frames are applied
in the order they are handed out, so the pending ones are those handed
out last.
